# Forever scoring (every class)

Item facts come from Wowhead Forever ingest. All nine classes are on this model. Re-score one class at a time after a rule change. Do not `reemit_all.py` from stale JSON.

## Levels 1–9

Every class, including paladin and warrior, is scored for levels 1–9 for both factions, one row per spec. The files are `Data.CLASS.Early.1to9.generated.lua`, emitted by `emit_early.py` with both factions. Do not put a hand-written Alliance mail list back into `Data.lua`. Do not emit `Data.Paladin.Horde.1to9` or `Data.Warrior.Horde.1to9`. Do not run `emit_horde19.py` for those classes. Rows still in `Data.lua` are Forever pins for the other classes. Paladin and warrior do not have those pins.

## Do not invent an item

Wowhead Forever is the client. If the Forever item page does not list an item, or `https://nether.wowhead.com/tooltip/item/{id}?dataEnv=16` is a 404, that item does not exist in Forever yet. Do not add a hunt for it. Do not write a name, a stat, or a tooltip for it from classic Wowhead, from dataEnv 1 or dataEnv 8, or from the unversioned nether tooltip. Those are a different game. A missing Forever item stays out of the addon until Forever lists it.

## Ranks close up

Removing an item never leaves a hole in the numbers. If rank 1 and rank 2 are gone, the next piece becomes rank 1, then rank 2, then rank 3. The hunt rows and the (i) list are the same pieces in the same order. A slot says it is empty only when nothing legal is left for that faction, class, spec, and level. Hiding a piece at display time is not enough when the stored band only kept the removed names. The next real piece has to be scored into that band.

## Weapons filter

Every class has one **Weapons** header. The arrow on that title, before the (i), is the filter. The header reads **Weapons*** while any checkbox in that filter is off. Two-hand, Main Hand, and Off Hand are checkboxes, and any of them can be on. For every spec that can dual wield, including a rogue, a One-Hand weapon belongs to both Main Hand and Off Hand. Main-hand-only weapons stay under Main Hand. Off-hand-only weapons stay under Off Hand. Unchecking a hand hides the weapons that only that hand can wear. A One-Hand weapon stays on the hunt while the other hand still allows that type. For every class, the printed weapon damage is the damage in either hand. A tooltip that says Main Hand stays out of the off hand. A tooltip that says Off Hand stays out of the main hand. A spec that cannot dual wield has no Off Hand section, and Main Hand keeps the one-handers. Under each hand is that hand's weapon types (dagger, sword, mace, axe, fist, polearm, staff). A type is listed only under the hand that still has a weapon of that type after the source filter and the play style. A hand with nothing left is not listed. Turning those back on brings the hand and the type back, still checked or unchecked as they were. Every listed type starts checked. Unchecking a type under one hand hides that type for that hand. A tooltip that glues the type onto the hand line (`One-HandMace`, `Main HandAxe`, `Two-HandSword`) is still that type. A weapon whose type cannot be read stays out once any type box is off. A single type must not hide a better weapon by being the only one on. The styles and the types are the ones in the current level band for this faction, class, and spec. Earlier levels are not listed.

Shields and held-in items keep their own slot. Ranged keeps its own slot, including wands and thrown. Hunters have a filter on the Ranged header, before the (i): Bow, Gun, and Crossbow, only the types in the current band that still pass the source filter and the play style, each starting checked. A type with nothing left is not listed, and it comes back with the same check when those filters allow it again. The header reads Ranged* while any of those is off. The filter only hides hunt rows. Rank numbers and the (i) list stay the unfiltered order. A type that cannot be read stays out once any box is off. The choice is remembered per class and spec. Other classes do not get that filter. Librams, totems, and idols stay in the relic slot. Fishing poles stay out. A weapon with no DPS is not in Weapons.

Right-click on the paper doll shows the active filter. Two-hand opens the main-hand slot only. Main Hand opens the main-hand slot. Off Hand opens the off-hand slot when that spec can equip a damaging weapon there. **Hunters can dual wield**, so a hunter Off Hand filter opens the off-hand slot. **Shamans cannot dual wield.** Enhancement is two-hand only. Elemental, restoration, and enhancement tank keep the shield on the off-hand slot. Rogues and fury warriors can dual wield. A shield spec still opens the shield list from the off-hand slot.

Main-hand-only weapons stay out of the off-hand list. Off-hand-only weapons stay out of the main-hand list. A unique weapon is not rank 1 in both hands. Before a hunter learns Dual Wield at 20, a One-Hand weapon is worn in the main hand and is ranked there from the level it can be equipped. After 20 it is the off hand. A weapon that earned a place on a hand stays through its item level, so a quest reward does not vanish the next level. Past that item level it stays only when its score still puts it in the top of that hand. A weak quest dagger does not linger as a deep off-hand rank.

The filter only hides pieces. Rank numbers stay the unfiltered order. If the best weapon is a two-hander, it stays rank 1 when two-handers are hidden, and a one-hander keeps its own number. It does not become rank 1. Recommended still means that unfiltered rank 1, and it only shows when that piece is inside the filter. The (i) list is that same unfiltered order, the 30 highest-scored weapons in the current level band, whatever the weapon filter is set to. A weapon scored below that stays in the item data and is left out of the addon, so a later weight change can score it back in. A hidden spell is not a hunt. Invisible Dagger (280304) is spell 1308095, a hidden dummy aura. An item whose name is UNUSED, (DNT), or Placeholder is the same kind of record and stays out of the addon. The six Leather Armor chests and the six Leather Pants (Brawler's, Defender's, Totemic, Trapper's, Stormrider's, Wisdom's) are bought from Pawani at the Barrens camp and Daniel Stitchsong at the Redridge camp, not from the Orgrimmar or Stormwind leatherworking trainer. The six leather helms stay boss drops of the Leatherworking pattern. The description says the pattern drops from those bosses and shows the Leatherworking skill line. The helm is crafted. The hunt rows are the filtered pieces, with those same numbers. The hunts-per-slot count is inside the active filter, so narrowing to daggers still shows up to that many daggers when they exist, each with its unfiltered rank. The choice is remembered for that class and spec. A filter set while simulating is remembered only for that simulated spec. It does not apply to another spec you simulate, and it does not apply after simulation is turned off. The first view follows that spec's weapon style: two-hand for a hunter, a feral druid, an arms warrior, a retribution paladin, or enhancement; Main Hand and Off Hand for a rogue or a fury warrior; Main Hand for a shield spec. Elemental, restoration, holy, and shockadin start with two-hand and Main Hand both on, because a staff or another two-hander and a one-hander are both worn. Holy and Shockadin keep two-hand swords, maces, axes, and polearms beside the shield. Restoration keeps staves, including Odo's Ley Staff, and the other two-handers a shaman can equip. Rogues include one-hand axes. Assassination, combat, and subtlety all list the one-hand types a rogue can equip. The spec does not lock the main hand to daggers. Combat still scores the axe highly. Axe of the Deep Woods is a main-hand axe on the combat list. A priest, a mage, a warlock, and a balance or restoration druid start with two-hand and Main Hand both on, so the one-hander that pairs with the off-hand stays on the list beside the staff. A type the new spec cannot use is dropped when you switch. Shield and held-in slots stay visible when Weapons is set to two-hand. The paper doll opens the off-hand for a weapon when Off Hand is checked and that spec can dual wield.

The source filter and a play style apply together with the weapon filter. Hunt rows, the shield list, and the character-panel bar hide a weapon that fails any of those. Rank numbers and the (i) list stay the unfiltered order of the current band. Changing the source filter or a play style drops the cached weapon hunt rows and does not drop the rank cache. The Off Hand shield and held-item list is cached apart from those rows. Drop that list in the same pass, or a Boss drop filter keeps the world-drop shields from the previous filter. If the parchment is open on a hunt the filter or a play style just hid, close it. The green arrow stays on the unfiltered best piece when the weapon filter hides it, and on every hunt still showing. The paper doll follows the weapon filter for a hunter, a rogue, and enhancement as well. Toggling a weapon checkbox clears the paper-doll upgrade cache and rebuilds the arrows. Do not clear that cache on every refresh, and do not nil the slot rank cache.

**Order:** damage (spec primary) first, then survivability (sta/armor through 60), then endurance (mana keep-up, less than stam). Level 60 uses the same survivability and endurance multipliers as 1–59. The hunt list shows 3 unique names per slot until the player raises **Hunts per gear slot** (3 to 10, saved on the account). The list updates when the slider is released. The number is how many hunts that slot still shows after the source filter and the play style, skipping pieces already obtained or removed. Rank numbers stay the real ones. Weapons stay one header. The character-panel bar, the obtain toast, and Recommended stay on rank 1, 2, and 3. A green arrow is only on a piece that is an upgrade, including a later rank that is on the list and beats what is worn. An empty slot counts. Simulation has no worn comparison, so the listed hunts keep the arrow there. The (i) list is unchanged. There is no notable shelf. A piece that used to be a notable takes its score rank with the rest, including rank 4 and later on the rank list.

## Do not change weights unless asked

`weights.json`, the numbers in `score.py` `weights_at_level()`, and `ScoringWeights.generated.lua` stay until the user explicitly asks for a retune. A piece ranking first because of an existing weight is the model working.

Look up a proposed weight change in the community sheet [GearQuest Forever - Class Stat Weights](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1jMC89KgzHpPZHYgcjOq6ef7lENhh8OGgypG2rC2m0Ok/edit). One tab per class, using that class's log scene. The live grid is the raw `weights.json` row (not pre-multiplied). Those cells are locked to Henrik. Anyone with the link writes in **Suggest a change** at the bottom (name, spec, stat, weight, why). A suggestion does not change the addon. Copy an accepted row into `weights.json` and re-score that class only when the user asks.

Priest, mage, and warlock `dpsWeightRanged` is **7** (same scale as a warrior melee weapon). These classes cast, then wand. **White Obsidian Wand** (274425) is rank 1 ranged at 39 for mage, priest, and warlock because 7 × 41.47 DPS wins the slot. Do not lower that weight to bury it. Greater Magic Wand stays the client 17.5 DPS. Warlock schools and Enhancement Tank armor (0.20, 0.40 below 60) stay as they are.

Holy paladin levels in melee. The scored row in `weights.json` is what the tooltip and the hunt use. Below 60, stamina, health, and health per 5 are ×3, armor is ×2, and intellect, spirit, mana per 5, and mana are ×2. The tooltip prints two decimals, so armor **0.004 × 2 = 0.008** shows as **0.01**. Healing is **1.3**, generic spell power **1.35**, holy spell power **1.8**, intellect **0.85** (shown **1.7**), spirit **0.3** (shown **0.6**), mana per 5 **1.1** (shown **2.2**), mana **0.04** (shown **0.08**), crit **0.8**, stamina **0.3** (shown **0.9**). A note that says healing **1.8**, intellect **1.2**, spirit **1.5**, and armor **0.15** (raw **0.075**) was never copied into that row. Do not retune the row to match the note. `+Holy Spell Damage` is stored as `spHoly` and, for Holy only, stays equal to the holy spell power weight **1.8**. Cloth `armorClass` is **0.62**, leather **0.82**, mail **0.96**, plate **1.0**. Do not flatten the cloth penalty so tailoring robes outrank blacksmithing mail and plate healing. Protection paladin armor is **0.2** (shown **0.4** below 60). Its `armorClass` is plate **1.0**, mail **0.55**, leather **0.35**, cloth **0.18**. The ×2 on the tooltip is the leveling armor stat, not a reason to wear cloth. Protection generic spell power is **0.9** and `spHoly` is **0.05**. Do not copy the Holy equality rule onto Protection. Protection mana per 5 is **0.2** (shown **0.4** below 60). Ranged attack power is **0** on every paladin spec, including levels 1–9. Shockadin is spell Retribution and is not a healer. Spell power is **1**, holy spell power **0.85**, intellect **0.4** (shown **0.8**), and crit **0.55**. Strength is **0.2** and weapon damage is **0.4**, so a swing still counts and a spell-power weapon stays ahead. Stamina is **0.15** (shown **0.45**), armor **0.004** (shown **0.01**), spirit **0.1** (shown **0.2**), mana per 5 **0.2** (shown **0.4**), and mana **0.03** (shown **0.06**). Healing is **0**. Cloth armorClass stays **0.62**, the same plate-caster curve as Holy. Both weapon styles start on.

